content.content:
Rebased onto `main` after #2714 and the branch is clean again. Worth noting that it collided on exactly the line your description predicted: the suppression attribute I had added sits on the first line of this file, and the sweep put the BOM there. The seven `PSUseBOMForUnicodeEncodedFile` findings that were left over are gone with #2714, so the widened set now reports nothing.

That closes everything open on my side: thirteen pull requests, no unresolved review threads, no failing checks.

---

Since this is a good place to stop, something I have not said directly.

I use Maester on client tenants, not as a lab exercise. Some of them run it on a weekly job so they can see their posture move — the value turns out to be less in any single result than in the shape of the series: what was red in March, what somebody quietly turned off in June. That is also why I keep landing on the same kind of finding here, the ones where a check reports something it does not actually know. A test that says "pass" when it means "I could not look" is worse than no test at all in that setting, because it is the one you stop reading.

That is what I like about this project, and about how you ran #2663: you asked for the measurement before the opinion, and when it turned out the convention and the enforcement were two different rules, you said so instead of papering over it. That is rarer than it should be.

Two practical things. I sometimes disconnect for a few days and step away from the computer, so if I go quiet on a thread that is all it is — my socials are on my profile if anything is ever urgent. And if there is anywhere else you want a hand, say the word: reviewing other people's pull requests, measuring something across the tree, or testing a branch against a production tenant rather than a lab. I am at your disposal.

